WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.435
A check-then-use gap appears when code
validates or canonicalizes a path,

2
00:00:04.515 --> 00:00:08.200
filesystem state changes, and a later call
opens or modifies the name.

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.777
If another actor can replace an
intermediate directory or link during that interval,

4
00:00:12.857 --> 00:00:15.855
the checked pathname may no longer reach
the same object.

5
00:00:15.935 --> 00:00:18.621
The earlier validation was accurate at its
moment;

6
00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:22.074
it did not permanently bind the future
operation to that object.

7
00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:24.277
The first mitigation is architectural:

8
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:28.542
keep protected storage outside directory
trees writable by untrusted users or

9
00:00:28.622 --> 00:00:29.996
less-trusted processes.

10
00:00:30.076 --> 00:00:32.575
Minimize the steps between resolution and
use,

11
00:00:32.655 --> 00:00:36.777
and avoid resolving the same external
reference through separate code paths.

12
00:00:36.857 --> 00:00:39.543
Where the runtime and operating system
support it,

13
00:00:39.623 --> 00:00:42.371
open with flags that reject unwanted link
behavior,

14
00:00:42.451 --> 00:00:44.450
use exclusive creation for new files,

15
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.215
and continue work through an already-open
handle.

16
00:00:47.295 --> 00:00:49.232
Understand each primitive's scope:

17
00:00:49.312 --> 00:00:52.435
a no-follow flag may address only the
final path component,

18
00:00:52.515 --> 00:00:54.576
and availability differs by platform.

19
00:00:54.656 --> 00:00:59.403
For stronger requirements, use
platform-specific directory-handle or sandbox designs

20
00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:02.418
rather than claiming a portable string
check is atomic.

21
00:01:02.498 --> 00:01:04.247
Creation needs particular care.

22
00:01:04.327 --> 00:01:07.513
Canonicalize and authorize an existing
controlled parent,

23
00:01:07.593 --> 00:01:09.591
generate the final name on the server,

24
00:01:09.671 --> 00:01:12.419
and create without overwriting an existing
object.

25
00:01:12.499 --> 00:01:16.747
Keep upload staging private and move into
final storage under controlled rules.

26
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:21.074
Restrict the service account and
filesystem access-control lists so a mistaken

27
00:01:21.154 --> 00:01:23.090
target cannot reach the entire host.

28
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:27.730
Permissions are defense-in-depth; they do
not excuse an application that computes an

29
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:28.872
unauthorized path.

30
00:01:28.952 --> 00:01:32.387
Destructive operations require a narrower
contract than reads.

31
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:36.152
Delete, overwrite, rename, recursive
removal, archive extraction,

32
00:01:36.232 --> 00:01:40.542
and temporary-file replacement can affect
more than one object or erase recovery

33
00:01:40.622 --> 00:01:41.121
options.

34
00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:45.698
Resolve exact targets, reject root
equality when the operation must address a child,

35
00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:48.964
avoid broad recursive calls on
request-derived locations,

36
00:01:49.044 --> 00:01:51.605
and verify operation-specific
authorization.

37
00:01:51.685 --> 00:01:55.620
A containment helper written for
downloading one file should not be reused

38
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.136
automatically for deleting a directory
tree.

39
00:01:58.216 --> 00:02:02.900
The operation, filesystem state, and
consequences determine the required safeguards.
